1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:03,580
One of the most famous bridges in the world.

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:07,440
An icon for the city of London for more than a century.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:12,120
A unique blend of modern engineering and Victorian aesthetic.

4
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:16,660
Now, Tower Bridge on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,120
In today's cultural and architectural landscapes dominated by rapid change and globalization,

6
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:46,860
there are a handful of monuments and buildings that stand out.

7
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,100
They have acquired a status that goes beyond their mere form or purpose.

8
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:58,120
They have become the symbols for their cities, their time, their cultures.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Its long history has awarded the City of London many remarkable structures.

10
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:12,000
But together with Big Ben, the famous clock tower flanking the Houses of Parliament,

11
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:17,000
the most recognized symbol for this city is the Tower Bridge.

12
00:01:17,001 --> 00:01:20,000
Revered by Londoners, loved by tourists,

13
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:26,000
the bridge on the eastern end of London's inner city is perhaps the most photographed bridge in the world.

14
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:37,300
Tower Bridge is a combination of marvellous feats of engineering with a magnificent, monumental Victorian architectural structure.

15
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:45,120
So the two coming together are much more than the sum of the parts, and this is what gives this Great Bridge landmark quality.

16
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:53,580
Tower Bridge was called the Wonder Bridge because of the way it was automated and opened to let ships pass through.

17
00:01:53,581 --> 00:01:58,420
It's only when you see the machinery and you understand how it worked that you appreciate

18
00:01:58,421 --> 00:02:04,300
the enormity of what it was for the people that designed it in the 1890s.

19
00:02:04,301 --> 00:02:08,920
Although more than a hundred years old, Tower Bridge is a relative newcomer among London's

20
00:02:08,921 --> 00:02:09,920
bridges.

21
00:02:09,921 --> 00:02:16,760
The history of bridging the Thames goes back almost 2,000 years.

22
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:21,440
Invading Roman armies constructed the first timber bridge near their settlement, Londinium,

23
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:24,440
around 70 A.D.

24
00:02:24,441 --> 00:02:30,440
In 1176, construction began on the first stone bridge across the Thames.

25
00:02:30,441 --> 00:02:36,440
It would be the city's most famous landmark for centuries, London Bridge.

26
00:02:36,441 --> 00:02:41,440
Completed in 1209, the long-awaited bridge quickly became popular,

27
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:45,440
not only as a means to cross the river, but as a place to do business,

28
00:02:45,441 --> 00:02:48,440
even as a place to live.

29
00:02:48,441 --> 00:02:54,680
The original London Bridge was quite a remarkable bridge and it went through a whole range of evolutionary stages,

30
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:59,620
from being just a plain road bridge to then having houses built on it.

31
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:01,820
It had a chapel built on it.

32
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:03,820
It had a gatehouse.

33
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,700
The bridge didn't have a regular pattern of arches.

34
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,240
It was very irregular.

35
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:15,600
And the reason it was irregular is when they built it, they built the piers on parts of the riverbed that were hard.

36
00:03:15,601 --> 00:03:18,820
they poked the bottom with large poles

37
00:03:18,821 --> 00:03:20,720
and where it was solid they built a pier.

38
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:23,900
It acted actually as a Thames barrier.

39
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,060
It was such an obstruction to the river

40
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:28,580
that at all states of the tide

41
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:32,000
there was a four to five foot difference in level

42
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:33,200
across the bridge.

43
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,940
The drop in water level created by the bridge

44
00:03:36,941 --> 00:03:38,940
made it virtually impossible to go upstream

45
00:03:38,941 --> 00:03:42,460
even for boats small enough to pass through the narrow arches

46
00:03:42,461 --> 00:03:46,240
and even going where the current was a risky undertaking

47
00:03:46,241 --> 00:03:48,840
that became known as shooting the bridge.

48
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:54,720
For six centuries, London Bridge remained the only way to cross the Thames

49
00:03:54,721 --> 00:03:56,180
other than by boat or ferry.

50
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,820
At the dawn of the Industrial Age, trade boomed in London.

51
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,300
England was becoming a major colonial power

52
00:04:06,301 --> 00:04:09,280
with territories and trade interests around the world

53
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:12,400
and an increasing flow of goods arriving at her port.

54
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:19,460
Beginning in 1729, a number of new bridges were erected in London and neighboring communities,

55
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:23,460
but all were west or upstream of London Bridge.

56
00:04:23,461 --> 00:04:30,460
In the early 19th century, London's population passed the million mark.

57
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:37,460
It would quadruple within 50 years, making London four times as populous as Paris or New York City.

58
00:04:37,461 --> 00:04:44,460
In 1831, a new London Bridge was completed right next to its crumbling predecessor,

59
00:04:44,461 --> 00:04:48,460
which was torn down after the new crossing was taken into service.

60
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:53,460
The new bridge did little to ease congestion.

61
00:04:53,461 --> 00:04:58,460
There was of course no other bridge to the east of London Bridge.

62
00:04:58,461 --> 00:05:02,460
And to the east is where a lot of the city was starting to grow,

63
00:05:02,461 --> 00:05:06,460
so that more than a million people lived to the east of London Bridge.

64
00:05:06,461 --> 00:05:08,780
and that was the only way that they could cross the river.

65
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:15,420
So it was jam-packed with horses and carts and people and hand carts and animals all being driven across.

66
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:19,660
So there was a real desperate need to have some other river crossing.

67
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,840
Though the need for a crossing east of London Bridge was obvious,

68
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:26,460
building another bridge seemed out of the question.

69
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:31,980
Because to the east of London Bridge began what is called the Pool of London,

70
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,100
an undistinguished stretch of river barely a mile and a half long.

71
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:44,060
Today, the few remaining warehouses that line the Pool of London house loft apartments for affluent Londoners.

72
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:53,800
In the 1800s, they were at the center of the world's busiest port, with a turnover of up to 300,000 tons of goods per week.

73
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:58,980
At times, more than 20,000 workers were employed on the docks and wharves.

74
00:05:58,981 --> 00:06:06,876
If you ever see the photographs of the Upper Pool of London with all the ships in it, you could almost walk across the river on the shipping.

75
00:06:06,877 --> 00:06:09,340
There was that many ships in the Upper Pool.

76
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:16,640
Trade companies that had invested large sums in the docks were among the strongest opponents of a bridge to the east.

77
00:06:16,641 --> 00:06:25,860
To have built an ordinary bridge across the river at the point where tower bridges would have destroyed the docks further up,

78
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:32,820
because most ships were sailing ships, so none of them could have got through, so all those docks would have fallen into disuse.

79
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:41,980
Another group fiercely opposed to the bridge project, to any bridge project in fact, were the owners and operators of ferries across the Thames.

80
00:06:41,981 --> 00:06:48,980
Facing such opposition, a bridge downstream from London Bridge seemed unlikely.

81
00:06:48,981 --> 00:06:54,980
Many of London's bridges were privately owned and levied tolls from pedestrians and vehicles.

82
00:06:54,981 --> 00:06:59,980
Needless to say, these tolls were not very popular with the general public.

83
00:06:59,981 --> 00:07:06,980
In the late 1870s, the Metropolitan Board of Works, a governmental body for the County of London,

84
00:07:06,981 --> 00:07:13,540
spent about 1.4 million pounds to purchase 11 bridges and make them toll-free crossings.

85
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,960
The Metropolitan Board of Works, London's central government,

86
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:23,580
had used ratepayers' money to buy all the London bridges from the companies

87
00:07:23,581 --> 00:07:27,160
across the Thames in central London and free them from tolls.

88
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:32,300
And once that had been done, people living east of London Bridge

89
00:07:32,301 --> 00:07:36,080
made lots of demands for their money to be used to give them a crossing.

90
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:40,900
In addition to pressure from the public, the docks and wharves themselves,

91
00:07:41,320 --> 00:07:43,780
the same interests that opposed a bridge in the past,

92
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,800
were the cause of an increasing need for a new crossing.

93
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:53,680
It had become apparent that the vast quantities of food and merchandise arriving at the docks

94
00:07:53,681 --> 00:07:56,920
had to be transported through the congested streets of London

95
00:07:56,921 --> 00:08:01,020
and across London Bridge, where they were often delayed for hours.

96
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:03,800
It was the docks that changed everything.

97
00:08:03,801 --> 00:08:11,040
Here was a great city investment of capital into a wet dock system down below London Bridge,

98
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:17,040
and getting access for goods in and out of London and across the Thames became a very important issue.

99
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:22,420
Not so much for social reasons for the people, except it provided employment, of course, in the docks.

100
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:26,900
With interest in a new crossing east of London Bridge renewed,

101
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:33,080
it was now up to London's architects and engineers to develop ideas for this much debated project.

102
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:40,800
The location of Tower Bridge, right next to the historic Tower of London, was determined in 1876.

103
00:08:40,801 --> 00:08:45,800
The Corporation of London had created a special bridge or subway committee

104
00:08:45,801 --> 00:08:51,800
to select the site and evaluate proposals for a new Thames Crossing east of London Bridge.

105
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:58,800
After it was clear where the new bridge should be, the main challenge facing all the engineers and architects

106
00:08:58,801 --> 00:09:04,040
architects was how to get vehicles and passengers across the pool of London without impeding

107
00:09:04,041 --> 00:09:05,793
shipping traffic.

108
00:09:05,794 --> 00:09:12,700
In 1879, the Metropolitan Board of Works presented three designs by

109
00:09:12,701 --> 00:09:16,715
their own chief engineer, Sir Joseph Bazalgette.

110
00:09:16,716 --> 00:09:20,480
Out of the three proposals, one clearly stood

111
00:09:20,481 --> 00:09:21,181
out.

112
00:09:21,182 --> 00:09:28,200
It was a concept for a steel bridge, an elegant design that leapt across the Thames

113
00:09:28,201 --> 00:09:31,720
in one single elongated 850 foot arch.

114
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:37,640
A horizontal roadway was supported 65 feet above high water level.

115
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:44,280
The approaches consisted of one long straight approach to the north side

116
00:09:44,281 --> 00:09:46,160
and one spiral approach to the south.

117
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:49,760
Crucially important to this and all other bridge proposals

118
00:09:49,761 --> 00:09:51,640
was the gradient of the approaches.

119
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,280
The gradient reflects the relation between the height and the distance to reach that height,

120
00:09:56,281 --> 00:09:58,000
in this case, 1 to 40.

121
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:07,980
The horse was the motive power, either for omnibuses or cabs or freight-carrying carts.

122
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:10,740
So you always had to look at the gradient.

123
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:16,000
Horses were a very big issue 100 years ago because that was the means of transport.

124
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:21,900
Cargo drawn by horses and carts clearly is best if it's drawn on completely level ground.

125
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:26,860
The inherent problems in Bazalgette's designs that led to their eventual rejection

126
00:10:26,861 --> 00:10:30,200
were problems that would haunt all fixed bridge proposals.

127
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:36,260
To clear shipping traffic, the bridge had to cross the Thames at a fairly high level,

128
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:39,060
at least 100 feet above the high water mark.

129
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:43,580
But to get traffic to such a high level,

130
00:10:43,980 --> 00:10:48,060
the approaches would have to be extremely long in order to accommodate horse-drawn traffic.

131
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,380
The room for approaches, however, was very limited.

132
00:10:51,381 --> 00:10:57,160
The amount of land to take, particularly on the city of London side,

133
00:10:57,860 --> 00:11:00,720
meant demolishing very expensive property

134
00:11:00,721 --> 00:11:05,200
and was just ruled out on economic grounds rather than on technical feasibility.

135
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:08,860
One design attempted to solve the problem

136
00:11:08,861 --> 00:11:12,980
by installing large elevator platforms on each side of a high-level bridge.

137
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:18,700
You entered a lift at one bank, went up into a high-level lift,

138
00:11:18,701 --> 00:11:22,820
crossed the bridge and then entered another lift and went down the other side.

139
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:28,960
That would have been fine, but if you consider that now we have 40,000 vehicles a day crossing the bridge,

140
00:11:29,380 --> 00:11:32,900
no way could it have coped with the flow, not even in Victorian times.

141
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:37,920
Clearly to solve this dilemma, the bridge builders needed a better concept.

142
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:41,889
Two years prior to Bazalgette's proposal, designer F.J.

143
00:11:41,890 --> 00:11:44,700
Palmer had suggested a so-called duplex bridge.

144
00:11:44,701 --> 00:11:53,840
You had the bridge designed so that one part of it opened so a ship could enter and you still had a continuous road.

145
00:11:54,360 --> 00:12:02,781
Then the gate was closed which also contained a road behind the ship and the other side opened so the ship could then pass through and the road traffic would be

146
00:12:02,782 --> 00:12:03,482
maintained.

147
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:08,520
But that was considered to be too complicated and also not practical

148
00:12:08,521 --> 00:12:11,320
because of the high running speed of the tide.

149
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:15,680
It would have been difficult to have moored the ships and then let them release again.

150
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:19,660
Nevertheless, with a fixed bridge out of the question,

151
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,020
the duplex bridge design was a step in the right direction.

152
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:28,960
The bridge would have to be at a low enough level to allow for approaches of reasonable length and gradient.

153
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:33,260
And it would have to be movable to let ships pass unhindered.

154
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:45,360
Of the many proposals brought forward between 1877 and 1885, several took their cues from this idea, sometimes with rather unconventional results.

155
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:57,260
One design had two swing spans pivoting around two massive towers and a short tunnel with elevator access to be used when the bridge was open.

156
00:12:57,261 --> 00:13:03,260
Another truly bizarre design was not really a bridge at all.

157
00:13:03,261 --> 00:13:09,360
It was a pontoon-like road segment, 100 by 300 feet in size, that would roll across the

158
00:13:09,361 --> 00:13:15,160
Thames on a half dozen piers fitted with rolling pins.

159
00:13:15,161 --> 00:13:21,660
The designer estimated his rolling bridge would make the trip across every six minutes,

160
00:13:21,661 --> 00:13:25,320
always leaving a large portion of the Thames open for ships to pass.

161
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:31,460
In 1884, one proposal emerged as the frontrunner.

162
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:37,220
Why was this design chosen when there were some very good designs amongst the others?

163
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:41,780
The reason I think it's quite simple is that the corporation of the City of London

164
00:13:41,781 --> 00:13:45,520
had their own in-house city architect, Horace Jones,

165
00:13:45,521 --> 00:13:51,460
who had sketched out a rough bascule design, a slightly fanciful, whimsical design.

166
00:13:51,660 --> 00:13:56,820
But they then decided that they would fund the design and construction of the bridge,

167
00:13:57,100 --> 00:14:01,440
which of course means that he who pays the piper calls the tune.

168
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:08,860
Horace Jones, the man with the inside track, had submitted his design for a bridge in 1878.

169
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,500
But Jones was not only competing for a winning tower bridge design.

170
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:18,520
As city architect, he was also involved in evaluating the many proposals.

171
00:14:18,521 --> 00:14:27,640
Beyond his political advantage, Jones' design showed some merit in addressing the problems facing a bridge across the Pool of London.

172
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:37,880
His bridge consisted of two brick towers resting on massive piers and accessed via steel suspension bridges from the shore.

173
00:14:37,881 --> 00:14:45,120
Between the towers, an arch supported two chain-drawn bascule leaves

174
00:14:45,121 --> 00:14:49,800
Wing-like bridge segments that could be raised much like medieval drawbridges

175
00:14:49,801 --> 00:14:51,460
To let ships pass

176
00:14:51,461 --> 00:14:58,460
John Wolfberry, son of Sir Charlesberry, the designer of the Houses of Parliament

177
00:14:58,461 --> 00:15:02,160
Was assigned as the engineer to work on the bridge with Jones

178
00:15:02,161 --> 00:15:09,160
He soon helped eliminate a substantial flaw from the original design.

179
00:15:09,161 --> 00:15:14,160
The Central Arch demanded that tall-masted ships pass dead center.

180
00:15:14,161 --> 00:15:19,160
Any deviation from course could result in a collision with the span.

181
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:26,160
Wolfberry replaced the Central Arch with a horizontal, high-level dual walkway.

182
00:15:26,161 --> 00:15:30,280
pedestrians unwilling to wait for the bridge to close would have the option of

183
00:15:30,281 --> 00:15:34,920
riding up a tower in an elevator crossing the footbridge and descending on the

184
00:15:34,921 --> 00:15:40,020
other side. In purely reductionist engineering terms you don't need the

185
00:15:40,021 --> 00:15:43,500
towers because you can operate the basquals from the piers and have a low

186
00:15:43,501 --> 00:15:48,320
level roadway without having towers but once the this idea that you should

187
00:15:48,321 --> 00:15:53,120
maintain a free passenger flow across the river even when the shipping was

188
00:15:53,121 --> 00:15:57,920
passing through the bridge dictated of course that you had to have an upper bridge and that

189
00:15:57,921 --> 00:16:02,508
bridge had to be quite high to clear the masts of sailing ships.

190
00:16:02,509 --> 00:16:04,300
The modified design would

191
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:09,480
provide a clearance of almost 140 feet between high water level and the upper foot bridges

192
00:16:09,481 --> 00:16:11,550
when the bascules were raised.

193
00:16:11,551 --> 00:16:15,760
The support of the suspension chains and the central walkways

194
00:16:15,761 --> 00:16:19,532
raised another doubt about Jones original design.

195
00:16:19,533 --> 00:16:22,920
Towers built from brick as he had envisioned

196
00:16:22,921 --> 00:16:27,500
would not be strong enough to withstand the mechanical forces imposed on the bridge.

197
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:32,040
But the choice to include brick towers into the design

198
00:16:32,041 --> 00:16:34,680
was prompted by demands to give the bridge a look

199
00:16:34,681 --> 00:16:37,280
that would not clash with that of its neighbor,

200
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:39,920
the 800-year-old Tower of London.

201
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:44,980
Even Bazalgette, when designing his fixed steel spans,

202
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:47,420
had sought to make his bridge blend with the tower

203
00:16:47,421 --> 00:16:51,200
by adding quaint medieval details to approaches and piers.

204
00:16:52,920 --> 00:17:03,066
After the public had marveled at the filigreed structures made possible by building with iron and steel, a sort of aesthetic backlash had occurred and affected a

205
00:17:03,067 --> 00:17:06,280
change of architectural taste in Victorian England.

206
00:17:11,657 --> 00:17:22,851
There was a period in the middle of the 19th century when the great railway engine sheds and structures like the Crystal Palace, the site of the Great Exhibition in

207
00:17:22,852 --> 00:17:26,400
Hyde Park, were built out of exposed iron and steel.

208
00:17:26,401 --> 00:17:39,409
And it was towards the end of the century that there was a reaction to this, and people wanted to actually cover up the steelwork and the ironwork and see that as a

209
00:17:39,410 --> 00:17:45,200
skeleton, and then have artistic decoration and stonework on the outside.

210
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:54,080
To accommodate both structural needs and public taste, a crucial and far-reaching change was made to Joan's original design.

211
00:17:54,081 --> 00:18:01,080
The bridge and the towers would be built as a steel frame and then covered to modify its appearance.

212
00:18:01,081 --> 00:18:07,080
White Cornish granite and Portland stone were chosen for the cladding.

213
00:18:07,081 --> 00:18:15,080
Aside from its decorative purpose, the masonry would also protect the steel from the elements and subsequent corrosion.

214
00:18:15,081 --> 00:18:21,080
I think most engineers would have preferred a structurally honest, visible system.

215
00:18:21,081 --> 00:18:21,781
system.

216
00:18:21,782 --> 00:18:25,880
The aesthetic of this is all wrong and came in for a great deal of criticism.

217
00:18:25,881 --> 00:18:32,920
And here the actual structural form was a visual lie. It looks like stone towers and

218
00:18:32,921 --> 00:18:38,700
in fact the structural steel work inside, the engineering part rather than the stonemasons

219
00:18:38,701 --> 00:18:42,440
part was the piece that was doing the work.

220
00:18:42,441 --> 00:18:47,340
After a long debate Parliament set forth the dimensions of Tower Bridge and conditions

221
00:18:47,341 --> 00:18:49,580
for its construction in 1885.

222
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:55,700
The cost for materials in construction was estimated at 750,000 pounds.

223
00:18:56,920 --> 00:19:00,380
Finally, on June 21, 1886,

224
00:19:01,060 --> 00:19:03,940
the Prince of Wales performed the groundbreaking ceremony.

225
00:19:06,120 --> 00:19:10,000
Shortly thereafter, a workforce of more than 400 men

226
00:19:10,001 --> 00:19:11,760
started phase one of the project,

227
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:15,380
the construction of the largest piers in the world.

228
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:19,920
They would soon get a taste of the difficulties ahead.

229
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:27,480
The unique structure of Tower Bridge rests on two enormous piers,

230
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:31,040
180 feet long and 70 feet wide.

231
00:19:32,940 --> 00:19:36,740
Each of the piers has to support a weight of 70,000 tons.

232
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:42,960
The pier's size was not only dictated by the fact that they had to support the towers,

233
00:19:42,961 --> 00:19:46,960
They also had to have room for engines and counterweights.

234
00:19:46,961 --> 00:19:50,960
The piers that you might think are solid are in fact hollow.

235
00:19:50,961 --> 00:19:55,960
When you see the bridge go up, this weight descends into that counterweight chamber,

236
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:57,960
which is of cathedral dimensions.

237
00:19:57,961 --> 00:20:01,960
To support the bridge structure and withstand water pressure,

238
00:20:01,961 --> 00:20:05,960
but also to survive the chance encounter with an errant ship,

239
00:20:05,961 --> 00:20:08,960
the piers' walls had to be extremely strong.

240
00:20:08,961 --> 00:20:18,960
They would be 10 feet thick in the weakest spot on the side, and a sturdy 22 feet at the up and downstream corners.

241
00:20:18,961 --> 00:20:23,960
Work on the foundations and the piers began in 1886.

242
00:20:23,961 --> 00:20:27,960
When they were completed, they would be the largest piers in the world.

243
00:20:27,961 --> 00:20:34,960
To lay the foundations and build the piers, workers sank caissons onto the riverbed.

244
00:20:34,961 --> 00:20:36,048
the riverbed.

245
00:20:36,049 --> 00:20:42,160
These were large metal boxes, 57 feet tall and open at the top and at the

246
00:20:42,161 --> 00:20:42,861
bottom.

247
00:20:42,862 --> 00:20:49,560
The caissons penetrated the riverbed 26 feet deep into the underlying layer of

248
00:20:49,561 --> 00:20:54,934
clay. The water was pumped out and a layer of gravel removed.

249
00:20:54,935 --> 00:20:56,960
Then the lower part was

250
00:20:56,961 --> 00:21:00,273
filled with concrete to create a foundation.

251
00:21:00,274 --> 00:21:03,360
The upper caissons, which were eventually

252
00:21:03,361 --> 00:21:08,420
taken out formed a metal wall that held out the water while the piers were built from

253
00:21:08,421 --> 00:21:15,360
brickwork and granite. Unfortunately, the piers could not be built in one piece due

254
00:21:15,361 --> 00:21:18,640
to restrictions by the river authorities.

255
00:21:18,641 --> 00:21:24,020
The Thames conservators who managed the river made a very stringent restriction on operation

256
00:21:24,021 --> 00:21:29,860
that you had to keep 160 feet clear between these two caisson walls at all times during

257
00:21:29,861 --> 00:21:30,981
the construction.

258
00:21:30,982 --> 00:21:35,400
Now the spacing of them meant that you couldn't build the two piers

259
00:21:35,401 --> 00:21:37,891
completely in one case at a time.

260
00:21:37,892 --> 00:21:41,740
You had to split the pier longitudinally, build one

261
00:21:41,741 --> 00:21:44,085
half of it, and then move the case on over.

262
00:21:44,086 --> 00:21:46,320
An expensive and time-consuming business.

263
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:53,500
So time-consuming that it took four years just to build the piers, almost as long as

264
00:21:53,501 --> 00:21:58,880
the projected schedule for the entire project.

265
00:21:58,881 --> 00:22:04,380
After the piers had been completed, construction could finally begin on the steel superstructure,

266
00:22:04,381 --> 00:22:10,120
beginning with the 120-foot steel frames for the main towers.

267
00:22:10,121 --> 00:22:15,040
Each of the towers comprises of four octagonal columns.

268
00:22:15,041 --> 00:22:19,140
They support the weight of the suspension bridge at the top,

269
00:22:19,141 --> 00:22:22,320
and are linked together by the walkways.

270
00:22:22,321 --> 00:22:27,140
Amazingly, the bridge's entire steel structure, including the massive tower columns,

271
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:31,560
was made up of thousands of relatively small steel plates and beams,

272
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:34,340
weighing no more than five tons apiece.

273
00:22:35,740 --> 00:22:40,640
The stipulation was that the cranes could only handle a five-ton piece at a time.

274
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:42,640
The pieces had to be manageable.

275
00:22:44,060 --> 00:22:47,000
To piece together a rigid structure of this size,

276
00:22:47,001 --> 00:22:50,880
the builders needed a reliable technique to join the steel segments.

277
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:53,500
They chose to use rivets,

278
00:22:54,280 --> 00:22:58,280
and three million of them were used to fasten everything from the tower frames

279
00:22:58,281 --> 00:23:01,100
to the bascule girders and suspension chains.

280
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:08,300
Once the tower's main frames had reached their intended height,

281
00:23:08,301 --> 00:23:11,380
the difficult task of building the footbridges began.

282
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:16,500
The lattice box girders supporting and enclosing the walkways

283
00:23:16,501 --> 00:23:19,540
were cantilevered out from both towers simultaneously.

284
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:25,660
It was a slow and difficult process as the girders inched toward each other.

285
00:23:27,160 --> 00:23:30,380
Despite the fact that all the pieces had been test-fitted in advance,

286
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:33,920
some doubts remained that they would meet and match as intended.

287
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:38,700
Because the piers would settle into the river very slightly,

288
00:23:39,200 --> 00:23:43,160
and with the riveting system is not as good as nuts and bolts in terms of register,

289
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,640
and they had no means by laser beams to measure how accurate they were.

290
00:23:48,641 --> 00:23:52,580
So there was a bit of a praying hope, I think, that when they got to the middle,

291
00:23:52,660 --> 00:23:54,340
they did find that it all lined up properly.

292
00:23:55,560 --> 00:23:58,980
When the walkways of Tower Bridge finally joined above the Thames,

293
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:04,340
they turned this work in progress into the first bridge ever downstream of London Bridge.

294
00:24:06,100 --> 00:24:10,180
With the walkways completed, attention focused on the two suspension bridges

295
00:24:10,181 --> 00:24:11,760
from the riverbanks to the towers.

296
00:24:13,560 --> 00:24:18,560
On top of the temporary wooden bridges that spanned the Thames between the piers and the riverbanks,

297
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:22,240
large wooden trestles and scaffolds were erected,

298
00:24:22,241 --> 00:24:26,640
leading upward to the main towers and the smaller abutment towers.

299
00:24:26,641 --> 00:24:33,640
On these trestles, workers assembled the massive stiffened suspension chains,

300
00:24:33,641 --> 00:24:35,640
weighing as much as a ton per foot.

301
00:24:35,641 --> 00:24:41,640
They were led from the main towers over the abutment towers to shore,

302
00:24:41,641 --> 00:24:46,640
where the chains were anchored in huge 30 by 50 foot concrete blocks.

303
00:24:46,641 --> 00:24:51,700
While the construction of the towers and the suspension bridges was challenging,

304
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:56,620
steel suspension bridges have been built on a larger scale since the 1830s.

305
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:03,980
At the center of Tower Bridge's unusual design was the double-leaf bascule bridge.

306
00:25:04,720 --> 00:25:08,180
No one had ever attempted to build a movable bridge of this size.

307
00:25:09,740 --> 00:25:15,520
The bascules, French for seesaws, are the movable bridge spans balanced with a counterweight.

308
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:23,640
Each of Tower Bridge's bascules would weigh 1,045 tons.

309
00:25:23,641 --> 00:25:34,640
When these enormous spans were assembled, restrictions to keep the waterway clear of obstructions made it necessary to adopt an unusual mode of construction.

310
00:25:34,641 --> 00:25:40,060
When the bascules were made for the bridge, because they didn't want to impede the river traffic,

311
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:45,200
the girders were put in in the upright position and the work was done upright,

312
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:49,440
even down to putting the wooden blocks, which were going to be the surface of the bridge,

313
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:52,400
in place with the bascules in the upright position.

314
00:25:53,940 --> 00:25:58,100
With most of the steel work in place, it was time for the stonemasons to go to work.

315
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:02,300
Despite the fact that the bridge structure is supported by steel frames,

316
00:26:02,980 --> 00:26:06,480
the designers opted to use massive blocks of a special granite

317
00:26:06,481 --> 00:26:08,640
instead of thinner tiles for the cladding.

318
00:26:10,560 --> 00:26:16,180
This nod towards solid construction weighed in at more than 30,000 tons from masonry alone.

319
00:26:16,980 --> 00:26:19,080
The white stone is called Portland Stone,

320
00:26:19,260 --> 00:26:22,000
and the reason it's called Portland Stone is it actually comes from a place called Portland,

321
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:24,580
which is on the Dorset coast.

322
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:30,580
It's a form of limestone, and it's very good for its hard-wearing properties,

323
00:26:30,581 --> 00:26:35,500
properties and it's also relatively easy to carve so that was a stone that was used to carve

324
00:26:35,501 --> 00:26:38,838
into the ornate tattens that you see on the bridge.

325
00:26:38,839 --> 00:26:41,980
The bridge's original architect Sir Horace Jones

326
00:26:41,981 --> 00:26:47,620
had died in 1887, too early to even see the foundations of his last work completed.

327
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:54,980
In his place, his assistant George Daniel Stevenson took over the project's architectural

328
00:26:54,981 --> 00:26:57,740
aspects and added some alterations of his own.

329
00:27:00,220 --> 00:27:04,580
Stevenson changed the original red brick facade to a white stone and granite look

330
00:27:04,581 --> 00:27:08,080
and modified windows, walkways, and parapets.

331
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:14,700
As the outer structure and the bridge spans took shape, a different kind of construction

332
00:27:14,701 --> 00:27:17,076
work was conducted on the inside.

333
00:27:17,077 --> 00:27:20,820
In cavernous chambers and engine rooms, an ambitious

334
00:27:20,821 --> 00:27:23,125
this engineering project was underway.

335
00:27:23,126 --> 00:27:26,340
Workers installed magnificent machines that would use

336
00:27:26,341 --> 00:27:30,180
fire and water to hopefully bring Tower Bridge to life.

337
00:27:33,640 --> 00:27:38,000
When the builders of Tower Bridge pondered the various power systems that might drive

338
00:27:38,001 --> 00:27:43,400
their bridge, they knew every one of their choices would have to hold up to one key requirement,

339
00:27:44,360 --> 00:27:46,120
absolute reliability.

340
00:27:46,121 --> 00:27:53,120
The project had prevailed against strong opposition because they had promised that river traffic would not be obstructed.

341
00:27:53,121 --> 00:28:02,120
Nobody wanted to be responsible for failing machinery that could turn a movable bridge into a barrier in the middle of the world's busiest port.

342
00:28:02,121 --> 00:28:16,121
When it was proposed that Tower Bridge should open by means of two bascules, there was a lot of derogatory comment that would these flaps always work, and they were

343
00:28:16,641 --> 00:28:19,120
port. talked about as flaps.

344
00:28:19,121 --> 00:28:24,120
Machine failure was not the only concern that the engineers needed to address.

345
00:28:24,121 --> 00:28:29,120
The collapse of Scotland's Tay Bridge due to high winds in 1879,

346
00:28:29,121 --> 00:28:34,120
in which 75 people had died, was fresh on the minds of government regulators

347
00:28:34,121 --> 00:28:41,120
when they decreed that bridge structures should be able to withstand wind pressure of 56 pounds per square foot.

348
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:48,120
Despite the fact that a wind of such strength would be highly unlikely within the city of London,

349
00:28:48,121 --> 00:28:53,460
of London. Engineers had to make sure that their bascules would close even if a wind

350
00:28:53,461 --> 00:28:57,402
of 87 miles per hour blew directly at them.

351
00:28:57,403 --> 00:29:01,160
While in theory several means of moving a

352
00:29:01,161 --> 00:29:07,360
bascule bridge were available, including hand operated cranks, the necessary high gear ratios

353
00:29:07,361 --> 00:29:11,235
would make most bridge lifts too slow to be practical.

354
00:29:11,236 --> 00:29:13,460
Once you've decided on having a

355
00:29:13,461 --> 00:29:18,040
lifting bridge, then the immediate technical question is how are you going to achieve that?

356
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:25,900
I suppose the greatest engineering technical feature of the bridge has to be the use of hydraulic power.

357
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:31,280
Hydraulics, the science of fluids in motion, was not new to the 18th century.

358
00:29:32,420 --> 00:29:35,500
Water-driven mills and pumps had been in use for centuries,

359
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:39,720
but it was not until the industrial age and the invention of steam engines

360
00:29:39,721 --> 00:29:45,100
that water could be pressurized and accelerated at much higher rates than its natural flow.

361
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,680
England was at the forefront of hydraulic engineering.

362
00:29:51,760 --> 00:29:56,700
In fact, in 1882, only four years before construction on Tower Bridge began,

363
00:29:57,740 --> 00:30:00,440
London had installed a system of hydraulic pipes

364
00:30:00,441 --> 00:30:05,940
that delivered pressurized water through street mains to workshops and office buildings

365
00:30:05,941 --> 00:30:10,440
where it could be used to operate machinery, cranes or elevators.

366
00:30:12,360 --> 00:30:15,120
To build the biggest hydraulic machine of its time,

367
00:30:15,640 --> 00:30:18,840
John Wolf Barry enlisted the services of Sir William Armstrong,

368
00:30:19,540 --> 00:30:21,100
one of the pioneers in the field.

369
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:23,940
William Armstrong was an inventor.

370
00:30:24,220 --> 00:30:25,220
He was a scientist.

371
00:30:25,880 --> 00:30:30,060
His own home had any number of hydraulic embellishments.

372
00:30:30,061 --> 00:30:33,960
He could move his flower pots in his conservatory.

373
00:30:33,961 --> 00:30:36,480
he could turn his spit hydraulically.

374
00:30:37,080 --> 00:30:39,880
The hydraulic system Armstrong and Barry developed

375
00:30:39,881 --> 00:30:42,380
would drive the bridge until 1976.

376
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:47,180
It was powered by two pairs of coal-fired boilers

377
00:30:47,181 --> 00:30:49,220
and two steam-pumping engines,

378
00:30:49,800 --> 00:30:52,500
situated in the main engine room under the southern approach.

379
00:30:54,260 --> 00:30:56,800
The engines themselves were very powerful.

380
00:30:56,801 --> 00:31:00,260
At the time, they put out 360 horsepower.

381
00:31:00,261 --> 00:31:03,980
they weigh overall about 40 tons.

382
00:31:04,460 --> 00:31:07,800
The flywheel on itself probably weighs about 12 tons.

383
00:31:08,520 --> 00:31:11,720
The flywheel itself is to maintain momentum of the system

384
00:31:11,721 --> 00:31:14,780
so that when a bridge diff was required,

385
00:31:15,060 --> 00:31:17,160
it could instantly speed up.

386
00:31:17,460 --> 00:31:19,440
It didn't have to start from scratch.

387
00:31:20,380 --> 00:31:23,420
While 360 horsepower may not seem much

388
00:31:23,421 --> 00:31:25,940
in the age of high-powered combustion in jet engines,

389
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:30,180
in the 1890s, these were among the most powerful engines available.

390
00:31:30,260 --> 00:31:37,260
The steam engines pumped out water at a pressure of 750 pounds per square inch.

391
00:31:37,261 --> 00:31:41,260
This pressurized water would be driven into six accumulators

392
00:31:41,261 --> 00:31:46,260
to store energy for moving the bridge.

393
00:31:46,261 --> 00:31:50,260
The accumulators were essentially vertical metal tubes.

394
00:31:50,261 --> 00:31:53,260
Two were housed near the machine room in the southern approach

395
00:31:53,261 --> 00:31:57,260
and two in each of the piers.

396
00:31:57,261 --> 00:32:05,160
A huge piston weighing 100 tons, as much as two battle tanks, acted as a pressurizing weight on top of each of the accumulators.

397
00:32:07,360 --> 00:32:14,060
The steam engines forced the pressurized water into the accumulators, pushing the pistons 35 feet up.

398
00:32:15,080 --> 00:32:19,020
Then, valves at the cylinder base were shut to prevent backflow.

399
00:32:19,780 --> 00:32:23,460
And the piston weights kept the water under pressure until needed.

400
00:32:23,461 --> 00:32:30,460
When a bridge diff was required and ready to go, the engine driver would open up a valve,

401
00:32:30,461 --> 00:32:36,460
which allowed the hydraulic water through the pipework, up over the bridge, down into

402
00:32:36,461 --> 00:32:40,460
the machinery rooms on the bridge and into the engines.

403
00:32:40,461 --> 00:32:46,460
There, the force of the water would activate the engine's pistons, which in turn began

404
00:32:46,461 --> 00:32:48,460
to move the crankshaft.

405
00:32:48,461 --> 00:32:57,060
The motion from the crankshaft turned a system of gear wheels, which locked with toothed quadrants on the back of the bascules and moved the bridge.

406
00:32:59,020 --> 00:33:04,800
To decrease the drive force needed and to take stress off the gears, the bascules were balanced.

407
00:33:07,260 --> 00:33:13,000
By this simple measure, the driving machinery does not have to move the entire weight of the bridge leaf.

408
00:33:13,580 --> 00:33:17,240
It basically tips the balanced bridge in the direction it is supposed to go.

409
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:25,360
The white section of the bascul we can see above my head is the balance weight, which is the rear end of the bascul, if you will.

410
00:33:25,980 --> 00:33:28,460
And that's the shorter section of the bascul.

411
00:33:29,100 --> 00:33:32,820
The blue section is just the supporting structure for the bascul.

412
00:33:36,260 --> 00:33:41,500
As the bridge is readied for a lift, hydraulic locks at the rear of the basculs are released.

413
00:33:41,501 --> 00:33:51,040
While the bridge swings upwards, the enormous counterweights descend into the cavernous 40-foot-high bascule chambers hidden inside the piers.

414
00:33:52,420 --> 00:33:58,720
To ensure reliable operation at all times, the hydraulic system was equipped with several levels of backups.

415
00:34:00,300 --> 00:34:05,620
Each of two twin Lancashire boilers could have provided sufficient power to drive the steam pumps.

416
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:08,420
Two pumps were housed next to the boilers.

417
00:34:08,421 --> 00:34:12,420
Again, each had enough power to pressurize the system.

418
00:34:12,421 --> 00:34:18,420
The hydraulic engines which turned the water pressure into the gear movement that drove the bascules,

419
00:34:18,421 --> 00:34:22,420
were housed in pairs inside the machine rooms.

420
00:34:22,421 --> 00:34:27,420
Each of the engines could raise or lower the bascule by itself.

421
00:34:27,421 --> 00:34:31,420
In addition, each engine room was duplicated inside the piers.

422
00:34:31,421 --> 00:34:34,420
It was built with considerable redundancy.

423
00:34:34,421 --> 00:34:38,100
So each of the bascues could be raised by one of four engines.

424
00:34:39,220 --> 00:34:43,740
So there were eight engines altogether, and only two of them were required to raise the bridge.

425
00:34:44,940 --> 00:34:49,760
As a final backup, the bridge was connected to London's new hydraulic main system,

426
00:34:50,240 --> 00:34:55,660
from where it could draw pressurized water on the off chance that all of its pumps and accumulators would fail.

427
00:34:58,060 --> 00:35:01,580
Installation of the many components of the hydraulic system of Tower Bridge

428
00:35:01,581 --> 00:35:06,440
took almost four years, from 1890 to 1894.

429
00:35:07,820 --> 00:35:11,700
After eight long years, the structure of the bridge was finally complete

430
00:35:11,701 --> 00:35:13,500
and its hydraulic system operable.

431
00:35:14,540 --> 00:35:18,000
Almost 1.2 million pounds had been spent on its construction,

432
00:35:18,580 --> 00:35:21,100
about $120 million in today's money.

433
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:25,140
Now the latest and most impressive addition to London's bridges

434
00:35:25,141 --> 00:35:26,820
was ready for service.

435
00:35:27,520 --> 00:35:29,680
And a royal welcome.

436
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:41,780
More than a century ago, on the last day of June 1894, the warehouses along the Pool of London were adorned in flags and banners.

437
00:35:42,680 --> 00:35:48,920
Hundreds of thousands of spectators lined the embankments and crowded the Thames in everything from barges to rowboats.

438
00:35:50,500 --> 00:36:00,000
The more fortunate waited in stands along the approaches, while an honor guard lined the length of Tower Bridge in expectation of the Prince and Princess of Wales.

439
00:36:01,580 --> 00:36:06,880
The Royal Cavalcade arrived at noon, and the Prince stepped up to a silver cup on a pedestal,

440
00:36:07,380 --> 00:36:10,320
a switching device installed for him to raise the bridge.

441
00:36:10,940 --> 00:36:14,620
The heir apparent turned the cup, and nothing happened.

442
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:19,300
There is a story that goes around that the only time the bridge failed to raise

443
00:36:19,301 --> 00:36:24,480
was on the opening day when the Prince of Wales turned the lever and the bridge didn't actually open.

444
00:36:25,200 --> 00:36:30,800
In actual fact, what happened was there was a time delay between him moving a handle,

445
00:36:30,801 --> 00:36:36,180
which was doing virtually nothing to the guys that were driving the bridge actually lifting it.

446
00:36:37,460 --> 00:36:42,240
After a few moments of anxious waiting, the bridge drivers finally received their signal,

447
00:36:42,840 --> 00:36:48,800
and under roaring cheers, the bascules lifted to let the first ship, the HMS Landraal, pass.

448
00:36:50,560 --> 00:36:54,160
With the festivities over, the bridge could settle into its new role.

449
00:36:54,161 --> 00:37:01,160
A dual role, as it soon became apparent, is both a hard-working bascule bridge and a landmark

450
00:37:01,680 --> 00:37:05,400
for the City of London.

451
00:37:05,401 --> 00:37:10,080
Tower Bridge's ascent as a symbol for the city was almost instantaneous.

452
00:37:10,081 --> 00:37:14,660
Visitors to London flocked to the new Wonder Bridge, as it was called, eager to observe

453
00:37:14,661 --> 00:37:18,060
the bridge in action.

454
00:37:18,061 --> 00:37:22,340
Most people were undeterred by an ongoing debate about the bridge's appearance.

455
00:37:22,341 --> 00:37:26,900
The trade publication The Builder blasted the design only weeks after the opening,

456
00:37:27,660 --> 00:37:33,900
accurately remarking, we declined to waste any plates in giving illustrations of the so-called architecture.

457
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:41,980
The observation by the building news that no real art can tolerate a casing was gentle by comparison.

458
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:48,560
People felt that it would have been much better had the engineering skeleton of the bridge shown through

459
00:37:48,561 --> 00:37:51,080
so that you saw the steelwork,

460
00:37:51,440 --> 00:37:54,560
and they felt that it was a sham to have covered it

461
00:37:54,561 --> 00:37:58,360
with baronial-style architectural stonework.

462
00:37:59,240 --> 00:38:01,180
It was innovative in the use of the steel,

463
00:38:01,660 --> 00:38:03,800
but then they covered that up,

464
00:38:04,120 --> 00:38:05,400
as though they were ashamed of it,

465
00:38:05,760 --> 00:38:08,460
with stone to match the tower.

466
00:38:08,920 --> 00:38:12,500
You have this sort of throwback to almost a Disney-like approach

467
00:38:12,501 --> 00:38:15,720
in making it look like a tower or a castle

468
00:38:15,721 --> 00:38:17,060
which it sat alongside.

469
00:38:18,560 --> 00:38:25,560
The discussion about the Tower Bridge's Gothic exterior was only a minor concern to the people who used it and those who operated it.

470
00:38:27,560 --> 00:38:38,560
In its first year of operation, an average of 8,000 horse carts and 60,000 pedestrians crossed the bridge each day, with an average 17 lifts for shipping traffic.

471
00:38:38,561 --> 00:38:43,940
According to the Act of Parliament of 1885 that allowed Tower Bridge to be built,

472
00:38:44,680 --> 00:38:48,540
shipping would always have the right-of-way and never be impeded.

473
00:38:49,060 --> 00:38:53,480
To facilitate this, the bridge had to be operational 24 hours.

474
00:38:54,320 --> 00:38:57,260
84 to 90 people used to work on the bridge.

475
00:38:57,261 --> 00:38:58,686
That was all trades.

476
00:38:58,687 --> 00:39:04,960
That was stokers in the boilers and people working in the workshops, the bridge drivers.

477
00:39:04,961 --> 00:39:08,500
each of the machinery rooms was manned 24 hours a day.

478
00:39:09,260 --> 00:39:14,920
Just in case a boat got stuck under the bridge, Tower Bridge had its own tugboat on permanent standby.

479
00:39:16,900 --> 00:39:21,380
In stables under the southern approach, the bridge also had a contingent of horses,

480
00:39:22,440 --> 00:39:24,540
a sort of 19th century tow truck.

481
00:39:25,460 --> 00:39:30,680
The bridge did own additional horses to assist heavy carts across the bridge

482
00:39:30,681 --> 00:39:35,160
or to remove obstacles like a horse that might have died on the bridge.

483
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:36,900
And that could delay a ship.

484
00:39:37,520 --> 00:39:40,140
There are references in the old incident books

485
00:39:40,141 --> 00:39:43,120
to horses having dropped dead in the middle of the bridge.

486
00:39:44,100 --> 00:39:46,980
For all the traffic that went across and under the bridge,

487
00:39:47,320 --> 00:39:51,840
one component that had been a major factor in the bridge's design was scarcely used.

488
00:39:52,380 --> 00:39:55,000
And then not always for the purpose intended.

489
00:39:56,300 --> 00:39:59,800
When the bridge opened, people were so fascinated by watching it lift.

490
00:39:59,801 --> 00:40:04,580
that they didn't bother to use the high-level walkways as a means of going across.

491
00:40:04,900 --> 00:40:07,460
They used them much more for entertainment,

492
00:40:07,800 --> 00:40:11,940
perhaps in an afternoon going and having a walk on the walkways of Tower Bridge.

493
00:40:12,360 --> 00:40:13,620
It was just a meeting place.

494
00:40:14,560 --> 00:40:16,360
Meeting places they became,

495
00:40:16,980 --> 00:40:19,940
particularly for lonely Londoners out looking for love.

496
00:40:20,640 --> 00:40:23,840
In those days, we were a 24-hour operation,

497
00:40:23,841 --> 00:40:28,620
and the ladies of the night used to come and ply their wares up there as well.

498
00:40:28,621 --> 00:40:33,680
And this wasn't thought by Parliament to be very good for the image of the bridge.

499
00:40:34,460 --> 00:40:38,320
Another attraction the Tower Bridge seems to share with many famous bridges

500
00:40:38,321 --> 00:40:41,220
is its appeal to those contemplating suicide.

501
00:40:42,340 --> 00:40:45,660
While today the walkways are sited with double-glazed windows,

502
00:40:46,240 --> 00:40:48,340
there was no such protection when the bridge opened.

503
00:40:49,220 --> 00:40:52,580
On numerous occasions, jumpers would climb through the latticework

504
00:40:52,581 --> 00:40:55,560
and leap 140 feet to their deaths.

505
00:40:55,561 --> 00:41:05,320
Right from the very word go, people would climb the stairs to the walkways and commit suicide.

506
00:41:06,160 --> 00:41:14,400
So in about 1908, they actually passed an act of parliament which said that the walkways must be closed.

507
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:19,020
And they remained closed until we became a tourist attraction in 1982.

508
00:41:20,620 --> 00:41:24,980
During World War II, German bombers rained destruction onto the city of London.

509
00:41:25,560 --> 00:41:30,560
Miraculously, Tower Bridge was unscathed by the London Blitz.

510
00:41:30,561 --> 00:41:35,560
It is widely believed that German bomber pilots preferred to keep the landmark intact,

511
00:41:35,561 --> 00:41:40,560
to use it as a marker for their bombing runs.

512
00:41:40,561 --> 00:41:44,560
After a brief resurgence of shipping traffic in the post-war years,

513
00:41:44,561 --> 00:41:50,560
the demise of the Port of London posed a different threat to Tower Bridge.

514
00:41:50,561 --> 00:41:56,600
The passage of ocean-going ships that required bridge lifts steadily declined in the 1960s,

515
00:41:57,180 --> 00:42:01,780
leading to a discussion whether Tower Bridge should become a permanently fixed bridge.

516
00:42:03,460 --> 00:42:09,420
In 1970, the Corporation of London decided to keep Tower Bridge as a moving bridge,

517
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:13,060
but to replace the old steam-driven hydraulic system.

518
00:42:13,061 --> 00:42:21,660
The number of ships coming up was such that it didn't warrant having the bridge operational 24 hours a day,

519
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:25,700
and therefore some other means of driving the bridge had to be found.

520
00:42:26,760 --> 00:42:29,400
The steam engines, of course, had to have continuous attention,

521
00:42:29,401 --> 00:42:34,460
and the idea was to change into an electric motor, which could easily be switched on and off.

522
00:42:35,680 --> 00:42:38,640
After more than 80 years of trouble-free service,

523
00:42:38,641 --> 00:42:43,480
the steam engines were replaced by electric motors powering an oil hydraulic system.

524
00:42:44,960 --> 00:42:47,600
The bridge retained a large part of its old machinery,

525
00:42:47,880 --> 00:42:50,260
which is now a major attraction in its own museum.

526
00:42:51,380 --> 00:42:56,160
And if the current bridge master has his way, it may someday be taken out of retirement.

527
00:42:56,900 --> 00:43:00,780
It's one of my long-term ambitions to get them running on steam again,

528
00:43:01,260 --> 00:43:02,480
which I think would be tremendous.

529
00:43:03,500 --> 00:43:06,620
We would have had a dual system probably with an electric motor.

530
00:43:06,621 --> 00:43:14,120
Even though more mundane machinery now does the work, and ships need a 24-hour notice to have the bridge lifted,

531
00:43:14,840 --> 00:43:18,820
Tower Bridge still opens up for shipping as it has for more than a century.

532
00:43:21,260 --> 00:43:23,900
Standby bridge staff, about to stop road traffic.

533
00:43:25,620 --> 00:43:30,260
And road traffic just has to wait, no matter who is in the car.

534
00:43:30,261 --> 00:43:37,060
We actually stopped Bill Clinton in his limo at the road gates just out here.

535
00:43:37,660 --> 00:43:39,780
Nobody actually told us that they was in the area.

536
00:43:40,300 --> 00:43:44,520
And I actually had the phone going in the cabin within a minute or two of starting the bridge.

537
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:47,640
We were Scotland Yard on the line saying, can you close the bridge please?

538
00:43:47,680 --> 00:43:48,780
We've got the president waiting.

539
00:43:49,840 --> 00:43:52,800
But that was too late. I'd started by then. The boat was starting to come through.

540
00:43:53,740 --> 00:43:58,660
Today the massive bascules of Tower Bridge are raised on average less than twice a day.

541
00:43:58,661 --> 00:44:06,160
The ocean-going freighters of old have long given way to tall, masted yachts and pleasure boats entering the upper pool.

542
00:44:07,780 --> 00:44:11,780
The Tower Bridge still shines as the proud portal to the City of London,

543
00:44:12,580 --> 00:44:16,320
a testament to technical ingenuity and the glory of a bygone era.

544
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:22,400
It welcomes all who arrive here, be it by ship or by tour bus.

545
00:45:27,440 --> 00:45:28,640
I'm out.

546
00:45:28,660 --> 00:45:29,500
Oh, my God.

